Identify libfabric senders by source address, not an 8-bit wire field.

NIXL 1.5.0's libfabric backend sends a transfer's notification before its
data writes complete. EFA delivers out of order, so the target counts each
transfer's writes before releasing the notification, and for that it has to
know which peer every write completion came from. It learned that from an
8-bit agent_idx field in the RDMA immediate data, so createAgentConnection()
refuses the 257th peer an agent ever connects to, and nothing frees a spent
index. A dKV agent in a 64-engine fleet spends them (CLIN-2055).

The receiver now takes the sender from the source address libfabric reports
on each completion (FI_SOURCE + fi_cq_readfrom), mapped to a peer through a
per-rail table filled in at fi_av_insert() time. The agent index becomes a
local uint32_t with no wire width, and the 256-peer check is gone.

This changes the libfabric wire format: the imm_data agent-index field is
reserved and sent as zero, and the handshake carries
NIXL_LIBFABRIC_PROTO_VERSION = 2, which rejects a version-1 peer at
connection setup. A patched agent cannot talk to an unpatched one, so every
node in a cluster has to move together.

This is a backport of ai-dynamo/nixl#2294, merged upstream after v1.5.0 as
c2f4580b18. The diff is that merge's, with only the trailing space on blank
context lines stripped by the repo's pre-commit hook.

TODO(GEX-4374): drop once the fix ships in a tagged NIXL release.

